    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d168">

                <pb facs="0452" n="424" xml:id="pg424"/>

                <head>168. Memorandum of Telephone Conversation Between the Under Secretary of State
                    (Ball) and the President’s Special Assistant for National Security Affairs
                        (Bundy)<anchor n="0" xml:id="d168-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" type="conversation-or-meeting-date"
                            when="1962-11-09T17:30:00-05:00">November 9, 1962, 5:30
                        p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>Ball told Bundy he hoped to get over to him by 10 a.m. tomorrow a
                        memorandum<anchor n="1" xml:id="d168-f2"/> which is a general kind of
                    planning memo which incorporates some views of the DOD which came out of their
                    meeting this afternoon.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d168-f3"/> It sets up various
                    courses of action based on pretty much the same assumption that we if we don’t
                    get the IL-28s out or any security against reentry, but that we take the
                    Russians at their word. It amounts to a consideration of direct military action
                    to achieve it or a continued system of surveillance with the expectation of Cuba
                    drawing fire and then taking some action on that. He is attaching a proposal of
                        Stevenson,<anchor n="3" xml:id="d168-f4"/> which Ball does not endorse, but
                    which he wanted the President to see on negotiating with Castro. It will give
                    good plane reading if he wants it.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
